  "warnings": [
    "Total vitamin C from food plus supplements should stay below 2000 mg per day for adults (US NASEM; ICMR-NIN upper limit not found). Many experts suggest under 1000 mg per day from supplements (UK NHS).",
    "Large doses can cause diarrhoea, stomach cramps and wind.",
    "Regular vitamin C supplements have been linked with higher kidney stone risk in men; people with a history of kidney stones or with kidney disease should check with a doctor first.",
    "People with iron-overload conditions (haemochromatosis, frequent transfusions) or G6PD deficiency should take vitamin C supplements only on medical advice.",
    "High-dose antioxidants may interact with chemotherapy or radiotherapy, statin plus niacin treatment, warfarin, oestrogen-containing medicines and aluminium antacids; ask the doctor or pharmacist.",
